insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('n-f-041','netplus','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the key difference between TCP and UDP?$$,$$TCP is connection-oriented and reliable, using a handshake and retransmission to guarantee ordered delivery. UDP is connectionless and unreliable but faster, with no delivery guarantees - used where speed matters more than guaranteed delivery (e.g., streaming, VoIP).$$),
('n-f-042','netplus','core',1,'1.2',$$What does a /26 CIDR notation mean in dotted decimal?$$,$$/26 means 26 network bits, equivalent to a subnet mask of 255.255.255.192 - leaving 6 host bits for 64 total addresses (62 usable) per subnet.$$),
('n-f-043','netplus','core',1,'1.3',$$What do the DNS record types A, AAAA, MX, and CNAME represent?$$,$$A maps a hostname to an IPv4 address. AAAA maps a hostname to an IPv6 address. MX specifies the mail server responsible for a domain. CNAME creates an alias pointing one hostname to another.$$),
('n-f-044','netplus','core',1,'1.2',$$What are the IPv6 address shorthand rules?$$,$$Leading zeros within each group can be omitted, and one contiguous run of all-zero groups can be replaced with a double colon (::) - but only once per address, to avoid ambiguity.$$),
('n-f-045','netplus','core',1,'1.4',$$What is the difference between a star and a full mesh network topology?$$,$$In a star topology, every device connects to a central point (like a switch); if the central device fails, all connectivity through it is lost. In a full mesh, every node connects directly to every other node, offering maximum redundancy at the cost of significantly more cabling.$$),
('n-f-046','netplus','core',1,'1.1',$$What does Software-Defined Networking (SDN) separate?$$,$$Separates the control plane (centralized decision-making in an SDN controller) from the data plane (distributed packet forwarding across switches), enabling centralized, programmable network management.$$),
('n-f-047','netplus','core',1,'1.1',$$What is Network Functions Virtualization (NFV)?$$,$$Replaces dedicated hardware network appliances (routers, firewalls, load balancers) with software-based equivalents running on standard servers, improving flexibility and reducing hardware dependency.$$),
('n-f-048','netplus','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the difference between static and dynamic routing?$$,$$Static routes are manually configured and do not adapt to network changes. Dynamic routing protocols automatically discover and adjust routes in response to topology changes, at the cost of additional configuration and router resource usage.$$),
('n-f-049','netplus','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the difference between distance-vector and link-state routing protocols?$$,$$Distance-vector protocols (like RIP) share their full routing table with directly connected neighbors and converge slowly. Link-state protocols (like OSPF) build a complete topology map and calculate the shortest path independently, converging faster.$$),
('n-f-050','netplus','core',2,'2.2',$$What is the purpose of creating VLANs on a switch?$$,$$Logically segments a single physical switch into multiple isolated broadcast domains, improving security and reducing broadcast traffic without requiring separate physical switches.$$),
('n-f-051','netplus','core',2,'2.3',$$Why should WEP be avoided as a wireless security protocol?$$,$$WEP has well-known, practically exploitable cryptographic weaknesses that allow attackers to recover the encryption key relatively quickly - WPA2/WPA3 should always be used instead.$$),
('n-f-052','netplus','core',2,'2.4',$$What is the difference between NAT and PAT?$$,$$NAT translates one private IP to one public IP (a 1:1 mapping). PAT (Port Address Translation) allows many private IPs to share a single public IP by mapping each internal connection to a unique port number - the common SOHO router setup.$$),
('n-f-053','netplus','core',2,'2.5',$$What distinguishes MPLS from typical consumer WAN connections like cable or DSL?$$,$$MPLS offers guaranteed bandwidth, low latency, and SLA-backed reliability suited to enterprise site-to-site connectivity, at a higher cost than best-effort consumer connections like cable or DSL.$$),
('n-f-054','netplus','core',3,'3.1',$$What is SNMP used for?$$,$$Simple Network Management Protocol polls network devices for performance and status information (CPU load, interface statistics, errors), enabling centralized monitoring and management.$$),
('n-f-055','netplus','core',3,'3.1',$$What is syslog used for?$$,$$A standard protocol for centrally collecting and storing log messages generated by network devices, servers, and applications - essential for centralized troubleshooting and auditing.$$),
('n-f-056','netplus','core',3,'3.1',$$What is NetFlow used for?$$,$$Collects and analyzes traffic flow data passing through a router or switch, supporting capacity planning, traffic pattern analysis, and anomaly detection.$$),
('n-f-057','netplus','core',3,'3.2',$$Why is establishing a network performance baseline important?$$,$$A baseline defines what "normal" performance looks like, so future deviations (latency spikes, unusual bandwidth usage) can be objectively identified rather than guessed at.$$),
('n-f-058','netplus','core',3,'3.3',$$What is the purpose of Quality of Service (QoS)?$$,$$Prioritizes latency-sensitive traffic (like VoIP or video conferencing) over less time-sensitive traffic (like bulk file downloads) when network bandwidth is contended.$$),
('n-f-059','netplus','core',3,'3.2',$$What is the difference between latency, jitter, and throughput?$$,$$Latency is the one-way (or round-trip) delay for data to travel. Jitter is the variation in latency over time, which is especially disruptive for real-time traffic like VoIP. Throughput is the actual achieved data transfer rate, as opposed to theoretical maximum bandwidth.$$),
('n-f-060','netplus','core',4,'4.1',$$What is the difference between RADIUS and TACACS+?$$,$$Both are AAA (Authentication, Authorization, Accounting) protocols. RADIUS combines authentication and authorization into one step and encrypts only the password. TACACS+ separates all three AAA functions and encrypts the entire packet body, offering more granular control.$$),
('n-f-061','netplus','core',4,'4.2',$$What is VLAN hopping?$$,$$An attack that exploits trunk misconfiguration or double-tagging to send traffic into a VLAN the attacker should not have access to, bypassing intended network segmentation.$$),
('n-f-062','netplus','core',4,'4.3',$$What risk does a rogue DHCP server introduce?$$,$$Can hand out malicious gateway or DNS settings to unsuspecting clients, redirecting their traffic through an attacker-controlled path (a form of on-path attack) without any obvious sign to the victim.$$),
('n-f-063','netplus','core',4,'4.4',$$What is the difference between a site-to-site VPN and a client-to-site VPN?$$,$$A site-to-site VPN permanently connects two office networks (e.g., branch to headquarters) at the gateway level. A client-to-site VPN connects an individual remote user's device to the corporate network on demand.$$),
('n-f-064','netplus','core',5,'5.1',$$What is a tone generator and probe used for?$$,$$Traces a specific physical cable through walls, conduits, or a punch-down block by injecting an audible tone at one end and detecting it with a probe at the other - useful when cables are unlabeled.$$),
('n-f-065','netplus','core',5,'5.1',$$What is the difference between a basic cable tester and a TDR (Time Domain Reflectometer)?$$,$$A basic cable tester verifies continuity and correct wiring (pinout). A TDR sends a signal down the cable and measures reflection timing to pinpoint the exact distance to a break or fault - far more precise for locating problems in long cable runs.$$),
('n-f-066','netplus','core',5,'5.2',$$What symptom indicates a duplicate IP address conflict?$$,$$Intermittent connectivity and explicit "IP address conflict" warnings on affected devices, since two devices cannot reliably share the same IP on a network simultaneously.$$),
('n-f-067','netplus','core',5,'5.2',$$What symptom indicates DHCP scope exhaustion?$$,$$New devices fail to obtain an IP address from DHCP and often self-assign an APIPA address (169.254.x.x), since the DHCP server has no addresses left in its configured scope to lease.$$),
('n-f-068','netplus','core',5,'5.3',$$What do nslookup and dig do?$$,$$Both query DNS servers directly to troubleshoot name resolution - checking what record a domain resolves to, whether a specific DNS server is responding, and diagnosing DNS-related connectivity issues.$$),
('n-f-069','netplus','core',5,'5.3',$$What does the arp -a command show?$$,$$Displays the local device's ARP cache, mapping known IP addresses to their corresponding MAC addresses on the local network segment - useful for diagnosing IP/MAC conflicts or spoofing.$$),
('n-f-070','netplus','core',5,'5.3',$$What does the netstat command show?$$,$$Displays active network connections, listening ports, and related statistics on a local machine - useful for identifying what a device is connected to or which ports are unexpectedly open.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('n-q-046','netplus',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$An application requires every packet to arrive in order, with automatic retransmission of any lost data. Which transport protocol should it use?$q$,$q$["TCP","UDP","ICMP","ARP"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$TCP is connection-oriented and guarantees ordered, reliable delivery via a handshake and retransmission mechanism, unlike UDP which offers no such guarantees.$q$),
('n-q-047','netplus',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$What is the dotted-decimal subnet mask equivalent of a /26 network?$q$,$q$["255.255.255.192","255.255.255.128","255.255.255.224","255.255.255.0"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$/26 means 26 network bits, leaving 6 host bits (2^6 = 64 addresses), which corresponds to a subnet mask of 255.255.255.192.$q$),
('n-q-048','netplus',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$A technician needs to configure DNS so that a domain resolves to its server's IPv6 address. Which record type should they create?$q$,$q$["AAAA","A","MX","CNAME"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$AAAA records map a hostname to an IPv6 address; A records serve the same purpose for IPv4 addresses.$q$),
('n-q-049','netplus',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$A company wants incoming email for their domain routed to their mail server. Which DNS record type must be configured?$q$,$q$["MX","A","TXT","NS"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$MX (Mail Exchanger) records specify which mail server is responsible for receiving email on behalf of a domain.$q$),
('n-q-050','netplus',1,'1.4','mcq',3,$q$A data center requires the highest possible redundancy between core switches, accepting significantly higher cabling cost and complexity in exchange. Which topology best fits this requirement?$q$,$q$["Full mesh","Star","Bus","Ring"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A full mesh topology connects every node directly to every other node, providing maximum redundancy at the cost of a rapidly growing number of required connections.$q$),
('n-q-051','netplus',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A company replaces dedicated hardware routers and firewalls with equivalent software running on standard servers. What is this approach called?$q$,$q$["Network Functions Virtualization (NFV)","Software-Defined Networking (SDN) exclusively","VLAN tagging","Port mirroring"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NFV specifically refers to replacing dedicated hardware network appliances with software-based equivalents on standard servers, improving flexibility and reducing hardware dependency.$q$),
('n-q-052','netplus',2,'2.1','mcq',3,$q$A routing protocol shares its entire routing table with directly connected neighbor routers, and takes a relatively long time to fully converge after a topology change. What type of routing protocol is this?$q$,$q$["Distance-vector (e.g., RIP)","Link-state (e.g., OSPF)","Static routing","Policy-based routing"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Distance-vector protocols like RIP share full routing tables with immediate neighbors and converge relatively slowly, unlike link-state protocols which build a complete topology map for faster convergence.$q$),
('n-q-053','netplus',2,'2.1','mcq',3,$q$A routing protocol has each router build a complete map of the network topology and independently calculate the shortest path to each destination. What type of routing protocol is this?$q$,$q$["Link-state (e.g., OSPF)","Distance-vector (e.g., RIP)","Static routing only","A default route configuration"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Link-state protocols like OSPF build a full topology map at each router, enabling faster, more accurate convergence than distance-vector protocols.$q$),
('n-q-054','netplus',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$A company wants to logically separate its accounting and engineering departments' traffic on the same physical switch, without installing separate physical switches. What should they configure?$q$,$q$["VLANs","A single flat network with no segmentation","A new WAN circuit","IPv6 tunneling"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$VLANs logically segment a single physical switch into isolated broadcast domains, achieving the desired separation without requiring additional physical hardware.$q$),
('n-q-055','netplus',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$A legacy wireless access point still offers WEP as a security option. Why should this be avoided in favor of WPA2/WPA3?$q$,$q$["WEP has well-known, practically exploitable cryptographic weaknesses that allow attackers to recover the key relatively easily","WEP is actually more secure but slower than WPA2","WEP requires more expensive hardware","WEP and WPA2 provide identical security"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$WEP's cryptographic weaknesses are well-documented and practically exploitable, making it unsuitable for any modern deployment compared to WPA2/WPA3.$q$),
('n-q-056','netplus',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A small office has 50 devices, each with a private IP address, sharing a single public IP address for internet access via unique port mappings. What feature enables this?$q$,$q$["PAT (Port Address Translation)","Static NAT (1:1 mapping)","DNS round robin","VLAN trunking"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$PAT maps many internal private IPs to a single public IP by assigning each connection a unique port number, which is exactly how most SOHO routers share one public IP among many devices.$q$),
('n-q-057','netplus',2,'2.5','mcq',2,$q$An enterprise needs guaranteed bandwidth and an SLA-backed reliability commitment connecting its branch offices to headquarters. Which WAN technology best fits this requirement over a typical consumer cable connection?$q$,$q$["MPLS","Standard residential cable internet","Dial-up","A basic DSL connection with no SLA"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$MPLS is specifically designed to offer guaranteed bandwidth, low latency, and SLA-backed reliability, which consumer-grade cable or DSL connections do not provide.$q$),
('n-q-058','netplus',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$Several nearby access points on the 2.4GHz band are configured on overlapping channels, causing interference. Which set of channels should be used to avoid overlap?$q$,$q$["Channels 1, 6, and 11","Channels 1 through 11 sequentially","Any channel above 20","Channel 1 only, regardless of AP count"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Channels 1, 6, and 11 are the standard non-overlapping channels in the 2.4GHz band, minimizing interference between nearby access points.$q$),
('n-q-059','netplus',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$A small branch office with a single, unchanging path to the internet uses a manually configured route to its ISP gateway rather than running a dynamic routing protocol. What type of routing is this?$q$,$q$["Static routing","Dynamic distance-vector routing","Dynamic link-state routing","Policy-based routing with BGP"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A manually configured, unchanging route is a static route - appropriate for simple topologies where the overhead of a dynamic routing protocol is unnecessary.$q$),
('n-q-060','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A network administrator wants to poll switches and routers for CPU load, interface errors, and status information from a centralized monitoring system. Which protocol should they use?$q$,$q$["SNMP","syslog","NetFlow","DHCP"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SNMP is designed to poll network devices for performance and status data, feeding centralized monitoring dashboards.$q$),
('n-q-061','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A company wants log messages from all its routers, switches, and servers centrally collected in one location for auditing and troubleshooting. Which protocol enables this?$q$,$q$["Syslog","SNMP","NetFlow","ARP"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Syslog is the standard protocol for centrally collecting log messages from many different network devices and systems.$q$),
('n-q-062','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A network team wants to analyze which applications and destinations are consuming the most bandwidth through a core router, to support capacity planning. Which technology should they use?$q$,$q$["NetFlow","Syslog","SNMP alone, without flow data","ARP"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NetFlow specifically captures and analyzes traffic flow data through a router, making it purpose-built for bandwidth usage analysis and capacity planning.$q$),
('n-q-063','netplus',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Before rolling out a major application update, an administrator records current network latency, bandwidth utilization, and error rates under normal conditions. What is this activity called, and why is it done?$q$,$q$["Establishing a baseline, so future deviations from normal can be objectively identified","Performing a penetration test, to find vulnerabilities","Running a vulnerability scan, to check for missing patches","Configuring QoS, to prioritize traffic"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A baseline captures what "normal" performance looks like, giving the team an objective reference point to detect future anomalies rather than relying on guesswork.$q$),
('n-q-064','netplus',3,'3.3','mcq',2,$q$A company's network becomes congested during video conferences, causing choppy audio while file downloads continue unaffected. Which technology should be configured to prioritize the real-time voice/video traffic?$q$,$q$["Quality of Service (QoS)","NAT","VLAN tagging alone","A static route"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$QoS prioritizes latency-sensitive traffic like VoIP and video conferencing over less time-sensitive traffic when bandwidth is contended, directly addressing choppy audio during congestion.$q$),
('n-q-065','netplus',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A VoIP call has an acceptable average delay, but the delay fluctuates unpredictably moment to moment, causing audio to sound garbled. Which network metric does this describe?$q$,$q$["Jitter","Latency","Throughput","Bandwidth"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Jitter is the variation in latency over time - even with acceptable average latency, high jitter causes exactly this kind of unpredictable, garbled real-time audio quality.$q$),
('n-q-066','netplus',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A connection is rated for 1 Gbps bandwidth, but a file transfer only achieves 400 Mbps in practice due to network overhead and congestion. Which term describes the actually achieved rate?$q$,$q$["Throughput","Theoretical bandwidth","Latency","Jitter"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Throughput is the actual achieved data transfer rate in practice, which is typically lower than the theoretical maximum bandwidth due to overhead, congestion, and other real-world factors.$q$),
('n-q-067','netplus',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$A data center deploys two core switches configured with a first-hop redundancy protocol so that if one switch fails, the other automatically takes over gateway responsibilities with minimal disruption. What design principle does this reflect?$q$,$q$["Eliminating a single point of failure through redundancy","Reducing cost by consolidating hardware","Increasing latency intentionally for traffic shaping","Simplifying the network by removing all redundancy"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Redundant core switches with a first-hop redundancy protocol (like HSRP/VRRP) eliminate the core layer as a single point of failure, maintaining connectivity if one device fails.$q$),
('n-q-068','netplus',4,'4.1','mcq',3,$q$A company wants an AAA solution that encrypts the entire authentication packet and separately controls authentication, authorization, and accounting as distinct, granular functions for network device administration. Which protocol best fits?$q$,$q$["TACACS+","RADIUS","Kerberos alone","LDAP alone"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$TACACS+ separates all three AAA functions and encrypts the entire packet body, offering more granular control than RADIUS, which combines authentication and authorization and encrypts only the password.$q$),
('n-q-069','netplus',4,'4.2','mcq',3,$q$An attacker exploits a switch's trunk misconfiguration and sends double-tagged frames to gain access to a VLAN they should not be able to reach. What attack is this?$q$,$q$["VLAN hopping","ARP spoofing","DNS poisoning","A rogue DHCP attack"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$VLAN hopping specifically exploits trunk misconfiguration or double-tagging to reach a VLAN outside the attacker's intended segment, bypassing network segmentation.$q$),
('n-q-070','netplus',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$An unauthorized device on a network begins issuing its own DHCP leases with a malicious default gateway address, redirecting victim traffic through an attacker-controlled path. What is this device called, and what type of attack does it enable?$q$,$q$["A rogue DHCP server, enabling an on-path (man-in-the-middle) attack","A legitimate DHCP relay agent, with no security impact","A DNS server, with no relation to DHCP","A NAT gateway performing its normal function"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A rogue DHCP server can hand out a malicious gateway or DNS setting to unsuspecting clients, redirecting their traffic through an attacker-controlled path without any obvious warning sign.$q$),
('n-q-071','netplus',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$A company permanently connects its branch office network to its headquarters network so that all devices on both sides can communicate transparently at all times. What type of VPN is this?$q$,$q$["Site-to-site VPN","Client-to-site VPN","A remote desktop session","A simple port forward"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A site-to-site VPN connects two networks together at the gateway level on a permanent basis, distinct from a client-to-site VPN which connects an individual remote user's device on demand.$q$),
('n-q-072','netplus',4,'4.4','mcq',2,$q$An individual remote employee working from home connects their laptop to the corporate network only while working, using VPN client software. What type of VPN is this?$q$,$q$["Client-to-site VPN","Site-to-site VPN","A leased line","MPLS"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A client-to-site VPN connects an individual device to the corporate network on demand, unlike a site-to-site VPN which permanently connects two entire networks.$q$),
('n-q-073','netplus',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$A technician needs to identify which of dozens of unlabeled cables in a wiring closet corresponds to a specific jack in an office. Which tool should they use?$q$,$q$["A tone generator and probe","A TDR","A multimeter","A packet sniffer"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A tone generator injects an audible tone at one end of a cable, which a probe can detect at the other end, letting a technician trace an unlabeled cable through walls or a punch-down block.$q$),
('n-q-074','netplus',5,'5.1','mcq',3,$q$A long cable run has a suspected physical break, and the technician needs to know the precise distance to the fault without physically inspecting the entire run. Which tool should they use?$q$,$q$["A TDR (Time Domain Reflectometer)","A basic continuity tester only","A tone generator and probe","A punch-down tool"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A TDR sends a signal down the cable and measures reflection timing to precisely calculate the distance to a break or fault, far more precise than a basic continuity tester.$q$),
('n-q-075','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$Two devices on the same network intermittently lose connectivity and both display "IP address conflict" warnings. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["Both devices have been assigned the same static IP address","The DHCP server is offline","A cable has a physical break","The devices are on different VLANs"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Duplicate IP address assignment (commonly from a static IP conflicting with the DHCP scope, or two static IPs colliding) causes intermittent connectivity and explicit conflict warnings on the affected devices.$q$),
('n-q-076','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$New devices joining a large office network fail to obtain an IP address and self-assign an APIPA address. Existing devices remain unaffected. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["DHCP scope exhaustion - the pool of available addresses has been fully leased out","The DNS server is offline","A duplicate IP address conflict","A VLAN misconfiguration on the core switch"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$When a DHCP server's scope runs out of available addresses to lease, new devices cannot obtain one and fall back to self-assigned APIPA addresses, while already-leased existing devices remain unaffected.$q$),
('n-q-077','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$A user can reach a server by its IP address but not by its hostname. Which command should a technician use first to diagnose the likely DNS issue?$q$,$q$["nslookup","arp -a","netstat","ipconfig /release"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$nslookup directly queries DNS to check whether the hostname resolves correctly, isolating whether the problem is DNS-related given that the IP-based connection already works.$q$),
('n-q-078','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$A technician suspects that two devices on the local network are conflicting over the same IP address and wants to see which MAC address the local machine currently associates with that IP. Which command should they run?$q$,$q$["arp -a","netstat","tracert","nslookup"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$arp -a displays the local ARP cache, showing the current IP-to-MAC address mappings the device has learned - directly useful for investigating an IP/MAC conflict.$q$),
('n-q-079','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$A technician wants to see which ports a compromised-looking workstation currently has open and listening, and what remote addresses it is connected to. Which command should they use?$q$,$q$["netstat","arp -a","nslookup","ping"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$netstat displays active connections and listening ports on a local machine, which is exactly the information needed to investigate suspicious network activity on a workstation.$q$),
('n-q-080','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',3,$q$Two devices on the same physical switch and same subnet cannot communicate, despite both having correct, non-conflicting IP addresses. What should a technician check next?$q$,$q$["Whether the two devices' switch ports are assigned to the same VLAN","Whether the devices have valid DNS records","Whether the ISP link is down","Whether the devices support IPv6"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$If IPs are correct but devices on the same physical switch cannot communicate, a VLAN mismatch between their switch ports is a common cause, since VLANs create separate broadcast domains regardless of subnet configuration.$q$),
('n-q-081','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$A laptop repeatedly disconnects from Wi-Fi whenever the user moves toward the far end of the building, near the edge of coverage. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["Weak signal strength/attenuation at the edge of the access point's coverage area","A DNS misconfiguration","A duplicate IP address","An incorrect default gateway"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Signal strength naturally weakens with distance and obstructions; disconnections specifically correlating with physical distance from the AP point to attenuation as the root cause.$q$),
('n-q-082','netplus',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$A user reports that pinging a server by its IP address succeeds, but browsing to it by name fails entirely. What category of issue does this point to?$q$,$q$["A DNS resolution issue","A physical cabling issue","A power supply issue","A VLAN trunking issue"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Since basic IP connectivity already works (confirmed by the successful ping), a failure specifically tied to using the hostname points to DNS name resolution as the problem.$q$),
('n-q-083','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',3,$q$After a switch firmware update, tagged VLAN traffic stops passing correctly across a trunk link between two switches, though access ports continue working normally. What should a technician check first?$q$,$q$["Whether the trunk port configuration (allowed VLANs, tagging/encapsulation) reverted or changed after the update","Whether the DHCP scope has available leases","Whether DNS records are correct","Whether the cable itself has failed physically"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A firmware update can reset or alter trunk port settings; since only tagged trunk traffic is affected while normal access ports still work, the trunk configuration itself is the most likely culprit.$q$),
('n-q-084','netplus',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$Multiple internal devices experience simultaneous ping timeouts to each other, but users can still browse external websites normally. What does this pattern suggest?$q$,$q$["A local switching/loop issue rather than an ISP or internet-related problem","The ISP connection is completely down","DNS servers are unreachable","The default gateway's public IP has changed"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Since external (internet) connectivity still works while internal device-to-device connectivity fails, the problem is isolated to the local network (such as a switching loop or misconfiguration), not the ISP link.$q$),
('n-q-085','netplus',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$When troubleshooting a reported connectivity issue, why should a technician test connectivity to the local default gateway before testing an external destination?$q$,$q$["It quickly determines whether the problem is local to the network or further upstream, narrowing the scope of investigation","Testing the gateway first is required by networking standards with no diagnostic benefit","External destinations should always be tested first for accuracy","Local gateway tests are irrelevant to the actual problem"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Testing the local gateway first efficiently isolates whether the issue is within the local network or further upstream, focusing subsequent troubleshooting effort in the right direction.$q$);
